# Bukkit listeners → NewListener + handlers

Do **not** add new classes that implement `Listener` and get registered with `registerEvents(...)` in `CivCraft` (or elsewhere) for normal gameplay events.

The project pattern is: **one** `NewListener` (`com.avrgaming.civcraft.listener.NewListener`) owns `@EventHandler` methods; feature logic lives in small handler classes under `listener/<eventname>/`.

**Copy `BlockBreakEvent` exactly.** Every event (dungeon, quest, guide NPC, etc.) uses the same shape: handler interface, one impl class per concern, `List<...Handler>` on `NewListener`, and a single `@EventHandler` that loops the list.

## Required pattern (canonical: `BlockBreakEvent`)

### 1. Handler interface in `listener/<eventname>/`

Package name = Bukkit event name in lowercase, e.g. `blockbreakevent`, `playermoveevent`, `playerharvestblockevent`.

```java
// listener/blockbreakevent/BlockBreakHandler.java
public interface BlockBreakHandler {
    void handle(BlockBreakEvent event);

    default boolean isCancelled(BlockBreakEvent event) {
        return event.isCancelled();
    }
}
```

### 2. One focused impl per feature/concern

```java
// listener/blockbreakevent/BlockBreakDungeon.java
public class BlockBreakDungeon implements BlockBreakHandler {
    @Override
    public void handle(BlockBreakEvent event) {
        // logic here — not delegated to a *Listener class
    }
}
```

Naming: `<Feature><EventShortName>` or `<Domain><EventShortName>` (e.g. `BlockBreakDungeon`, `DungeonPlayerMove`, `QuestDailyBlockPlace`).

### 3. Register in a `List<...Handler>` on `NewListener`

```java
private final List<BlockBreakHandler> blockBreakHandlers = List.of(
        new BlockBreakTownClaimed(),
        new BlockBreakStructure(),
        new BlockBreakDungeon());
```

Use a **separate list** when priority differs (e.g. `blockBreakHandlers` at `HIGHEST`, `blockBreakMonitorHandlers` at `MONITOR`). Do not mix priorities in one loop unless every handler shares that priority.

### 4. Exactly one `@EventHandler` per list (loop + optional early return)

```java
@EventHandler(priority = EventPriority.HIGHEST, ignoreCancelled = true)
public void onBlockBreak(BlockBreakEvent event) {
    for (BlockBreakHandler handler : this.blockBreakHandlers) {
        if (handler.isCancelled(event)) {
            return;
        }
        handler.handle(event);
    }
}
```

Match `priority` / `ignoreCancelled` to the handlers in that list (same as existing dungeon/quest handlers for that event).

## Forbidden patterns

```java
// ❌ BAD — separate Listener + CivCraft.registerEvents
public class DungeonListener implements Listener {
    @EventHandler
    public void onPlayerMove(PlayerMoveEvent event) { ... }
}

// ❌ BAD — “god” class with many onXxx methods, wired by direct calls from NewListener
public class DungeonListener {
    public void onMoveEvent(PlayerMoveEvent e) { ... }
    public void onTeleportEvent(PlayerTeleportEvent e) { ... }
}
// NewListener:
dungeonListener.onMoveEvent(event);  // NOT the BlockBreak pattern

// ❌ BAD — handler that only forwards to a god class
public class DungeonPlayerMove implements PlayerMoveEventHandler {
    public DungeonPlayerMove(DungeonListener listener) { ... }
    public void handle(PlayerMoveEvent e) { listener.onMoveEvent(e); }
}

// ❌ BAD — @EventHandler on NewListener with inline feature logic (belongs in handler impl)
@EventHandler
public void onPlayerMove(PlayerMoveEvent event) {
    if (!event.getPlayer().getWorld().getName().equals("world")) { ... }
}
```

```java
// ✅ GOOD — same as BlockBreakDungeon
public class DungeonPlayerMove implements PlayerMoveEventHandler {
    @Override
    public void handle(PlayerMoveEvent event) {
        // move/teleport logic (or call a non-Listener domain helper)
    }
}
```

## Shared logic across handlers

- **3+ handlers** or **non-trivial shared rules**: extract to an existing manager/repository or a **plain** domain type (e.g. `DungeonTeleportTransitions`) — **not** a class named `*Listener`, **not** `implements Listener`.
- **2 call sites**: small duplication or a package-private helper on the natural owner is fine (`prefer-few-service-classes.mdc`).

## Handler rules

- Handlers are **not** `Listener` implementations; they only implement `*EventHandler` and `handle(Event)`.
- Keep handlers small and single-purpose; **chain order matters** when handlers can cancel.
- Call existing managers/repositories from handlers.
- Never add `CivCraft.registerEvents(...)` for gameplay events covered by `NewListener`.

## Checklist before finishing listener work

1. New behavior is in `listener/<eventname>/<Name>.java` implementing `<Event>Handler`.
2. Handler is added to the correct `List<...>` on `NewListener`.
3. `NewListener` has one loop `@EventHandler` for that list (no direct calls to a `*Listener` god object).
4. No new `implements Listener` types and no new `registerEvents` in `CivCraft`.

## Exceptions

Only skip `NewListener` when there is a clear, existing exception (deprecated legacy listener being migrated, or code outside `civcraft` main plugin). For new CivCraft features, use `NewListener` unless the user explicitly approves another approach.

Third-party events (e.g. `CitizensEnableEvent`) still use the **same** handler list + loop on `NewListener`; only the handler package/interface name follows the event (e.g. `citizensenableevent`).
